# hledger CSV rules for Kardabra's transaction export.
#
#   hledger import --rules kardabra.rules kardabra-transactions.csv
#
# Save each new export over the old one, under the same name: `hledger
# import` remembers the newest date it imported per file name, so under a
# new name ("kardabra-transactions (1).csv") every row is imported again.
#
# A starting point: copy it, then change the account names to your own.
# Kardabra writes one row per card transaction, signed from the card's
# side (a purchase is negative, a payment or refund positive), so the
# amount below goes to the card's liability account as it stands.
#
# - Each card gets its own account, named after its nickname in Kardabra.
#   Renaming a card in Kardabra renames its account here; to keep accounts
#   fixed, match on %card_last4 instead (see the example at the end).
# - Bill payments go to a transfer account. Import your bank statement
#   with the other side of each payment to the same account, and it nets
#   to zero instead of counting the money twice.
# - Each statement's last row carries the balance the bank printed, as a
#   balance assertion: a missing or double-counted row shows up as an
#   error instead of drifting silently. `hledger import` itself doesn't
#   check assertions, so run `hledger check` right after importing.
# - Opening balances are not used here: enter the card's balance before
#   the first imported statement yourself (an opening-balance transaction).
# - Categories are yours to add: match %description_raw (the statement's
#   own text, never changed by Kardabra) or %merchant, as in the examples.

skip 1

fields id, date, posted, description_raw, merchant, amount, currency, kind, card_last4, card_nickname, card_issuer, statement_id, period, opening_balance, closing_balance, booked

date-format %Y-%m-%d

# Payee is the cleaned merchant name, the note keeps the raw statement text.
description %merchant | %description_raw

currency %currency
amount %amount

account1 liabilities:creditcard:%card_nickname
account2 expenses:unknown

# The bank's balance after this statement, on its last row only.
balance %closing_balance

# `booked` is the transaction date, except a charge bought before the
# previous statement closed but billed on this one, which moves to the day
# after that statement. Dating by it keeps each statement's balance check
# exact; the real transaction date stays as the secondary date (--date2).
date %booked
date2 %date

if %kind ^payment$
  account2 assets:transfer:card-payments

if %kind ^(fee|forex-markup)$
  account2 expenses:fees

if %kind ^interest$
  account2 expenses:interest

if %kind ^cashback$
  account2 income:cashback

# Examples to adapt (remove the leading "# "):
#
# if %merchant ^(Swiggy|Zomato)$
#   account2 expenses:food
#
# if %description_raw AMAZON
#   account2 expenses:shopping
#
# if %card_last4 ^1234$
#   account1 liabilities:creditcard:regalia
